        "followup": "Agitation management algorithm: SAFETY FIRST: ensure staff safety, remove dangerous objects, have adequate backup. TREAT REVERSIBLE CAUSES: check vitals (hypoxia, hypoglycemia), pain assessment, bladder scan (urinary retention), review meds (steroid psychosis, withdrawal, drug interactions), assess for delirium (ABCDEF bundle). VERBAL DE-ESCALATION: calm environment (dim lights, reduce noise), speak calmly and respectfully, give space, identify patient needs/concerns, reorient (clock, calendar, explain where they are), set limits clearly, offer choices when possible. INVOLVE FAMILY: familiar faces often calming, can help reorient. NON-PHARMACOLOGIC: frequent reorientation, sleep hygiene, early mobilization, hearing aids/glasses, avoid unnecessary lines (especially Foley), minimize restraints. PHARMACOLOGIC (if above fails): Haldol 0.5-2mg PO/IM (avoid if prolonged QT), risperidone, quetiapine (better in elderly/dementia). Benzos (lorazepam 0.5-1mg) if alcohol/benzo withdrawal, but worsen delirium otherwise. PHYSICAL RESTRAINTS: LAST RESORT, requires physician order, frequent monitoring (q15 min initially, q2h once calm), document indication and attempts at de-escalation, time-limited (must renew q4h), discontinue ASAP. Complications: pressure injuries, aspiration, DVT, death. Prevention better than treatment: CAM-ICU, multicomponent delirium prevention."
