        "followup": "Capacity assessment - four components: UNDERSTANDING: Can patient repeat back information? \"Tell me what I just explained about your condition and the proposed treatment.\" Test: explain diagnosis, treatment options (including risks/benefits/alternatives), consequences of refusing. APPRECIATION: Does patient recognize information applies to them personally? \"What do you think will happen if you don't take this medication?\" Can they appreciate consequences? Lack of insight \u2260 lack of capacity, but inability to appreciate situation suggests impaired capacity. REASONING: Can patient manipulate information rationally? \"How did you decide to refuse treatment?\" \"What are you weighing in your decision?\" Can compare options and explain reasoning. Needs logical thought process. COMMUNICATION: Can patient clearly communicate a choice? Must be consistent over time. Fluctuating decisions may suggest delirium impairing capacity. DOCUMENTATION: Note all four components assessed, patient's responses, your clinical judgment. Capacity is DECISION-SPECIFIC (patient may have capacity for some decisions but not others) and TIME-SPECIFIC (can fluctuate). PSYCHIATRIC ILLNESS does NOT automatically mean incapacity - many psychiatric patients retain capacity. If patient LACKS capacity: identify surrogate decision-maker (healthcare proxy, family per state law), use substituted judgment (what would patient want?) or best interests standard. PSYCHIATRIC HOLD vs CAPACITY: involuntary psychiatric commitment (danger to self/others, gravely disabled) is separate from medical decision-making capacity. Patient can be on psychiatric hold but still have capacity to refuse medical treatment."
