        "followup": "Hyperglycemia causes pseudohyponatremia: glucose draws water from cells \u2192 dilutional hyponatremia. Correction factor: For every 100 mg/dL glucose above 100, add 2.4 mEq/L to measured sodium (some use 1.6 for glucose >400). This patient: (720-100) = 620, 620/100 = 6.2, 6.2 \u00d7 2.4 = 15, corrected Na = 128 + 15 = 143 (actually HIGH). Implication: patient is actually hypernatremic despite measured hyponatremia. When treating hyperglycemia, expect measured sodium to RISE as glucose normalizes. Failure of sodium to rise appropriately suggests underlying hyponatremia. Other causes of pseudohyponatremia: severe hypertriglyceridemia (>1500 mg/dL), multiple myeloma (paraproteinemia) - both laboratory artifacts with indirect ISE method. Direct ISE or calculate osmolal gap to detect. True vs pseudo: measure serum osmolality - if normal (280-295), pseudohyponatremia likely."
