        "followup": "True hypocalcemia (ionized calcium <4.6 mg/dL or corrected calcium <8.5): Parathyroid causes - hypoparathyroidism (low PTH, high phosphate), pseudohypoparathyroidism (PTH resistance, high PTH, high phosphate). Vitamin D deficiency (low 25-OH-D \u2192 decreased Ca absorption \u2192 secondary hyperPTH \u2192 low/normal Ca, low phosphate). CKD (decreased 1,25-diOH-D production + phosphate retention \u2192 hypocalcemia). Hypomagnesemia (<1.0 impairs PTH secretion AND end-organ resistance). Acute pancreatitis (calcium sequestration in saponification). Tumor lysis (hyperphosphatemia \u2192 calcium precipitation). Hungry bone syndrome (post-parathyroidectomy - rapid bone uptake). Critical illness (ionized hypocalcemia common). Symptoms: perioral paresthesias, tetany, Chvostek/Trousseau signs, seizures, prolonged QT, laryngospasm. Treatment: symptomatic/severe (Ca <7.5 or QT >500): calcium gluconate 1-2g IV slowly, then infusion. Oral: calcium carbonate + calcitriol (if hypoparathyroidism or vitamin D deficiency). Always check and replete magnesium first."
