        "followup": "Contrast-induced AKI (CI-AKI): Cr rise \u22650.5 mg/dL or \u226525% from baseline within 48-72h of iodinated contrast (some definitions use \u22650.3 mg/dL). Peaks at 3-5 days, usually resolves within 7-10 days. Pathophysiology: direct tubular toxicity + renal vasoconstriction \u2192 medullary ischemia. Strongest risk factor: baseline CKD (risk 10-50% if CKD stage 4-5). Other risk factors: diabetes with CKD, volume depletion, CHF, high contrast dose (>100 mL), multiple exposures within 72h, concurrent nephrotoxins (NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, amphotericin), age >75, anemia, hypotension. Prevention: IV isotonic saline 1 mL/kg/h x 12h before and after (most effective!), minimize contrast volume, stop nephrotoxins, avoid dehydration. N-acetylcysteine controversial (likely no benefit). Sodium bicarbonate no better than saline. Hold metformin 48h if eGFR <60 (lactic acidosis risk). Consider alternative imaging (ultrasound, MRI without gadolinium) if high risk. Dialysis does NOT prevent CI-AKI."
