        "followup": "Post-transplant infection timeline: First MONTH (0-30 days): Donor-derived infections, surgical site infections, nosocomial bacteria (pneumonia, UTI, line infections, C. diff), wound infections, anastomotic leaks. Pathogens: MRSA, VRE, gram-negatives, Candida. Prevention: perioperative antibiotics, wound care, early line removal. 1-6 MONTHS: Opportunistic infections (peak immunosuppression). CMV (most common, 8-32% incidence - pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, retinitis), EBV (\u2192 PTLD risk), BK virus (renal transplant - nephropathy), HSV/VZV reactivation, PJP (if no prophylaxis), invasive fungal (Aspergillus, Mucor, endemic mycoses). Prevention: CMV prophylaxis (valganciclovir 3-6 months), PJP prophylaxis (TMP-SMX lifelong), screening. >6 MONTHS: Community-acquired (if good allograft function, stable immunosuppression) - typical CAP, UTI. Chronic viral infections (CMV, EBV, BK). Opportunistic if over-immunosuppressed or rejection treatment. Approach: high index of suspicion, broad initial coverage, invasive diagnostics (BAL, biopsy), adjust immunosuppression if feasible. Leading cause of death first year. After first year: malignancy and cardiovascular disease become leading causes."
