        "followup": "Maintenance immunosuppression - triple therapy standard: CALCINEURIN INHIBITORS: Tacrolimus (FK-506, Prograf): blocks IL-2 production by inhibiting calcineurin. First-line, more potent than cyclosporine. Trough level monitoring. Adverse: nephrotoxicity (dose-dependent), neurotoxicity (tremor, seizures, PRES), new-onset diabetes (20%), hyperkalemia, hypomagnesemia. Cyclosporine (Sandimmune, Neoral): similar mechanism. Level monitoring. Adverse: nephrotoxicity, hirsutism, gingival hyperplasia, hypertension. ANTIPROLIFERATIVES: Mycophenolate mofetil/mycophenolic acid (CellCept/Myfortic): inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase \u2192 blocks purine synthesis \u2192 inhibits T/B cell proliferation. Adverse: GI (diarrhea, nausea), leukopenia, increased infection risk. Azathioprine (Imuran): purine analog. Less used now. Adverse: bone marrow suppression, hepatotoxicity. CORTICOSTEROIDS: Prednisone: multiple mechanisms. Tapered over time, many centers use steroid-free protocols. Adverse: hyperglycemia, osteoporosis, weight gain, cataracts, impaired wound healing. MTOR INHIBITORS: Sirolimus/everolimus: alternative to CNI, steroid-sparing. Block mTOR \u2192 inhibit IL-2 response. Adverse: hyperlipidemia, poor wound healing, mouth ulcers, proteinuria. Drug interactions: CNIs metabolized by CYP3A4 - many interactions (azoles, macrolides increase levels; rifampin decreases). Monitor levels closely."
